Dependencies (direction): UI events → mutate S → save() + render*. Generation reads config + coverage + fatigue + fairness-history, writes S.assign, then validation/scoring judge it. Rendering reads everything but writes nothing except UI-local state (hoursOpen, newsIdx, modal contents). News and PDF are leaves (read state, no coupling back). Persistence is called directly by UI handlers — there is no store layer.

2. SCHEDULING ENGINE (what the code ACTUALLY does)
Pipeline: defaults() config → shiftRange() resolves each shift-row's wall-clock window → buildSchedule() fills S.assign row by row → validateSchedule() hard-gates each of 50 candidates → scoreSchedule() ranks survivors → best-of-50 → improve-only commit → snapshotFairness() → qualityModal(); failure → restore backup → rejectionNotice().

Shift templates. Six fixed rows (no UI to add/remove shifts): AM-FT, MID-FT, PM-FT, AM-PT, MID-PT, PM-PT, each {tkey, kind, emp, need:{role:count}}. Current needs: AM-FT 1 Lead+2 Bud, MID-FT 1 Bud, PM-FT 1 Lead+2 Bud, AM-PT 1 Bud, MID-PT 0, PM-PT 1 Bud = 7 Budtender slots/day = 49/week. shiftRange() priority: (1) S.times[day__shiftId] custom override, (2) SHIFTTPL[tkey] with Fri/Sat (day===4||5) weekend times, (3) fallback deriving from store open/close using FT_MIN=510/PT_MIN=390 (only reachable for non-template keys, which cannot currently be created). Closed day → null → row skipped in generation, and staffing a closed day is a violation.
Role eligibility. Exact match only: pick() requires s.role===role. Leads can only ever fill Lead-need slots, Budtenders only Budtender-need slots. All Managers are manual:true (enforced by v5 migration), and pick() excludes s.manual — so Managers are never auto-scheduled, ever. Manager presence comes only from hand-entered S.manual times or manual slot assignment.
FT/PT behavior. Coverage is role-based: roleBodies() counts bodies by role only; any FT/PT mix satisfies a headcount. FT/PT matters in exactly three places: (a) paidHours() charges the shift row's designation (FT_PAID=8, PT_PAID=6.5), not the worker's — a PT worker on an FT row costs 8 h against their cap; (b) sort preference in pick() (same-emp first, skipped when crossCover==="always"); (c) hard emp gate only when crossCover==="never".
Cross-cover. S.opt.crossCover: "never" = hard same-emp filter in pick(); "necessary" (default) = same-emp sorted first, cross-emp allowed (including fatigue fall-through); "always" = no emp preference. PT-designated rows are filled first (shOrder sorts PT rows first), so PT staff naturally claim PT-intended work before FT backfill.
Availability / days off. pick() excludes s.off days. Validation flags any assignment or manual time on an off day as a violation. Generation additionally requires fresh = not already working that day (assignedToday, which includes manual times) — one shift per person per day, hard.
Hour limits. pick() requires h[id]+paid<=s.max+1e-6 where paid is the row's paid value. Validation flags paidHours(s)>s.max. h[] is generation-local; paidHours() recomputes from live S.assign + manual times (manual: actual minutes minus 30-min lunch iff shift ≥8 h, rounded to half hours).
Rest/fatigue. fatigueOpt() defaults: min rest 11 h, close-to-open banned, max 5 consecutive days, max 3 consecutive nights, min 1 day off/week (all tunable in Settings; missing keys fall back to these defaults). personShifts() merges slot assignments (with that row's resolved times) and manual times (kind inferred from clock: start<12:00→AM, end≥20:00→PM, else MID), sorted chronologically. Generation trial-assigns then rejects the candidate person if fatigueIssuesFor().length>0 (push/pop probe). Validation reports every issue per person.
Manager coverage. managerOnDuty() = manual Managers whose time overlaps the row's window and who aren't off that day. A need.Manager>0 row fails with no manager on duty. (All default rows have need.Manager=0; v5 migration zeroes any legacy Manager needs.)
Lead coverage. leadWindow() builds coverage segments over the row's window from assigned Leads (full-row segments), manual Leads/Managers (clipped segments), and slot-assigned Managers (full-row). Any uncovered gap → Lead coverage gap … (no Lead or Manager present). This is continuous-coverage, not headcount.
Opening/closing substitution. budTolOK() (requires S.opt.mgrCovers, AM/PM rows only): AM = some manager overlapping with start before 9:00; PM = some manager covering past the row's midpoint. When true, generation decrements that row's Budtender want by 1 and shiftCoverage() forgives one miss (budSub=true), so both sides agree.
Manual/sacred assignments. S.manual[day__staffId]={s,e}. Skipped by the generator, counted by manualCover/roleBodies/managerOnDuty/leadWindow (overlap-clipped), still subject to day-off/fatigue/double-book validation. Generation also subtracts manualCover overlap from each row's wants before filling.
Labor budget. S.opt.laborBudget; blank/0/invalid = no cap (input handler deletes the key). validateSchedule() and renderIssues() flag Schedule requires X hours, but weekly labor budget is Y hours (over hour cap) when totalLabor()>B. The (over hour cap) suffix routes it into scoring's hours bucket (×10000). Rejection modal hoists budget lines to the top. It never steers construction — pure candidate filter.
Fairness scoring. shiftDesir: AM +1, PM −1, Sat −1, Sun −1.5. fairBal = lifetime + current desirability average, used as a sort key (direction flips by row desirability). scoreSchedule soft terms: hour-utilization stdev ×120, night-range ×60, morning/evening ×25, weekend ×40, consecutive-same-kind pattern ×12, cross-week history deviation ×5, shift-pref mismatch ×6, cross-cover χ² ×12, noise ×0.5. Hard terms (×10000): gaps, leadgap, fatigue, off, hours, overlap. Collected but weightless: sub/amGaps/amSubs/pmGaps/pmSubs/mgr (displayed in Quality modal, zero influence on selection).
Historical fairness. snapshotFairness() writes per-staff {pts,n,AM,MID,PM,WE,xc} under S.fairHist[weekStart] on every apply and every undo. fairTotals() merges all weeks (skipping deleted staff) plus live current-week data if unsnapshotted. xcStats() blends current-week FT→PT fills with historical xc averages into a χ² concentration metric.
Improve-only. best.score < cur (current schedule's score) → apply; else restore backup and show "Current schedule kept." Ties keep the old schedule.
Undo. lastGenBackup (assign+manual JSON, set at each generate) → restore + re-snapshot + renderAll. Single depth; overwritten on every generate.
3. SHARED LOGIC — duplication and disagreement risks
Single-source-of-truth wins first: shiftRange(), roleBodies(), budTolOK(), leadWindow(), personShifts(), fatigueIssuesFor(), paidHours() are each defined once and shared by coverage/validation/scoring/generation. Real divergences found:

Cross-cover counted three ways. xcStats() current-week = FT staff on PT rows only. renderIssues() mix line = same (FT→PT). But snapshotFairness() writes xc++ for any s.emp!==sh.emp (both directions, including PT→FT). Historical xc therefore mixes bidirectional counts into a metric whose current-week half is unidirectional — the χ² blends inconsistent definitions.
Two violation classifiers. scoreSchedule() classifies by substring ("hour cap", "day off", "double-booked", "Short:", "Lead coverage gap", …else fatigue); renderIssues() uses independent filter logic. A future message rewording can silently move a violation between buckets (e.g. dropping "(over hour cap)" would demote the budget from ×10000 to a fatigue-weight slap). The labor-budget string and the rejection-hoist filter ("weekly labor budget") are coupled by invisible convention.
paidHours charges row designation, not worker type — consistent everywhere it is used, but it is a semantic trap for any future module that assumes personal hours.
counts() is computed but unused in renderIssues and openPicker (dead variable, suggests a removed per-person display).
Dead render paths: renderDayGrid() and renderPerson() have no callers; renderGrid() unconditionally renders matrix view (S.opt.view is written but never read). shiftColor2/SHIFTC appear unused (matrix uses KINDGRAD).
